---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { pillars, compliance } from "../data/site.js";

const title = "Family Office Tax Services | Detroit & Denver | Advisant";
const description =
  "Consolidated tax planning across operating entities, holding companies, and trusts for families in Metro Detroit and Denver, from IRS-licensed enrolled agents.";
const fo = pillars[2];
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/family-office/">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="formline formline--onDark">Family office</p>
          <h1>One team holding the whole picture.</h1>
          <p>
            When a family's affairs span an operating company, a holding entity, a trust or
            two, and several states, the risk is rarely a single wrong number. It is that no
            one is looking at all of it at once.
          </p>
          <div class="hero__actions">
            <a class="btn" href="/contact/">New client application</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/tax-advisory/">Advisory services</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="credential">
          <div class="credential__seal" aria-hidden="true">EA</div>
          <h2>Built for complexity, not volume.</h2>
          <p>
            We take on a limited number of family engagements so that the person who knows
            your structure is the person answering the phone.
          </p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Scope</dt><dd>Entities, trusts, family</dd>
            <dt>Cadence</dt><dd>Quarterly, plus as needed</dd>
            <dt>Coordination</dt><dd>Attorney &amp; advisor</dd>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="formline">What it is</p>
        <h2>The problem is fragmentation, not arithmetic</h2>
        <p>
          Most families in this position already have good advisors. An attorney who drafted
          the trusts. An investment advisor managing the portfolio. A preparer filing the
          returns. Each is competent inside their own lane, and none of them owns the seams.
        </p>
        <p>
          The seams are where the money is. A distribution timed without reference to the
          owner's other income. A charitable gift made in cash when appreciated stock was
          sitting in the account. A trust that has been paying tax at compressed rates for
          years because no one modelled distributing income to beneficiaries instead. An
          entity that made sense at formation and has been quietly wrong since.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our role is to sit across those seams — to hold the consolidated view, to bring the
          other advisors into the same conversation, and to raise the question that falls
          between two people's remits.
        </p>

        <h2>What we handle</h2>
        <ul>{fo.points.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>

        <h2>Risk management, because wealth creates exposure</h2>
        <p>
          Families in this position face risks that scale with what they own. Wealth attracts
          claims. Structural complexity — an operating company, rental property, a boat, a
          teenage driver, a board seat — creates personal liability that sits outside the
          business and outside most people's coverage. Entity structure, umbrella coverage,
          and titling either contain that exposure or they do not, and most families have
          never had anyone look at all three together.
        </p>
        <p>
          We review where liability actually sits, and coordinate with your insurance counsel
          on the gaps. This is not investment risk; it is the kind that arrives as a lawsuit.
        </p>

        <h2>Planning across generations, not tax years</h2>
        <p>
          A family office exists because the planning horizon is longer than a filing season.
          The organising question is what transfers, to whom, and in what form — and every
          annual decision either serves that or works against it.
        </p>
        <p>
          That means gifting on a schedule rather than in a panic, using the annual exclusion
          and lifetime exemption deliberately, allocating GST exemption where it will do the
          most work, and structuring charitable giving so appreciated assets rather than cash
          fund it. It also means the operating business is prepared for transfer or sale years
          before either happens.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is not what you make. It is what you keep, and what reaches the next generation
          intact.
        </p>

        <h2>How engagements run</h2>
        <p>
          Family engagements are year-round and priced as such. We start with a structural
          review: every entity, every trust, the ownership chart, the last two years of
          returns for each, and a conversation with your attorney. That review usually
          surfaces two or three things worth acting on before it is finished.
        </p>
        <p>
          From there the cadence is quarterly, with estimated payments and cash-flow managed
          across the whole structure rather than entity by entity, and a planning session
          before year-end while elections are still available.
        </p>
        <p>
          We are enrolled agents, which means we can also represent any entity or family
          member before the IRS directly — in all 50 states, on returns we did not prepare.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="facts">
        <p class="formline">A fit if</p>
        <ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;font-size:.95rem">
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">You file returns for more than three related entities</li>
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">A trust holds an interest in the operating business</li>
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Your advisors do not talk to each other</li>
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Charitable giving is meaningful and unstructured</li>
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0">A generational transfer is in view</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start a conversation</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--sunk">
    <div class="wrap measure">
      <p class="formline">Important disclosures</p>
      <p class="disclosure">{compliance.rlaLine}</p>
      <p class="disclosure" style="margin-top:1rem">{compliance.taxSeparationLine}</p>
      <p style="margin-top:1.5rem;font-size:.85rem">
        <a href={compliance.advUrl}>Form ADV Part 2A</a> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
        <a href={compliance.crsUrl}>Form CRS</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
